{"id":"4718656e-f51f-4f00-aa8e-4a18b7a3a4d9","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885826,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    nodes = case.get(\\\"nodes\\\")\\n    required_symbols = case.get(\\\"required_symbols\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case structure\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    parent_map = {}\\n    for nd in nodes:\\n        nid = nd.get(\\\"id\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"id must be string\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = nd\\n        parent_map[nid] = nd.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for nid in node_map:\\n        parent = parent_map[nid]\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent in children:\\n                children[parent].append(nid)\\n            else:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"parent {parent} not found\\\")\\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, nd in node_map.items() if nd.get(\\\"parent\\\") is None]\\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\\n    req_syms_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    all_symbols = {nd[\\\"symbol\\\"] for nd in node_map.values()}\\n    for s in req_syms_set:\\n        if s not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {s} not present in any node\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nd in node_map.values():\\n        sym = nd[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda nd: (-nd[\\\"depth\\\"], nd[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = nd[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"symbol\\\"] in req_syms_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not nd.get(\\\"removable\\\", False):\\n            continue\\n        if any(child_id in retained for child_id in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        sym = nd[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        new_count = symbol_counts[sym] - 1\\n        if sym in req_syms_set and new_count == 0:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = new_count\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The case payload contains nodes (list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of strings). We need to perform grammar-guided node reduction (like Perses). The objective: reduce tree while preserving grammar validity (i.e., required symbols must be represented) and interestingness (preserving required symbols). We consider deeper nodes before shallower, tie-break by node ID. We can remove removable nodes, but cannot remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children (since removing a node would remove its children? Actually Perses can delete a node and promote its children? But here description: \"Never remove... a node with retained children.\" So only leaf nodes? Or internal nodes with no children? Wait: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" If a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. That suggests we can only remove leaves (nodes with no children) or nodes whose children are all removed. But the algorithm might consider removing a node and replacing it with a subtree? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So we are removing nodes; we are not replacing with other subtrees. The \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means we check after removal, the set of symbols present in the remaining nodes still contains all required_symbols.\n\nSo essentially, we have a tree. We need to decide which nodes to remove (only removable nodes) such that:\n- The root is always retained.\n- Nodes with required symbols are retained.\n- Nodes that are not removable cannot be removed.\n- After removal, the remaining nodes must still collectively have all required symbols present in their symbol fields.\n- The process: consider deeper nodes first (higher depth), then tie-break by node ID (string). For each removable node that is currently a leaf (or all its children have been removed? Actually we need to consider \"retained children\" - if a node has any child that is retained, we cannot remove it. So we can only remove nodes that are leaves in the current retained set, or nodes whose all children have been removed. Since we are processing in order, we can iteratively remove nodes that are removable, not root, not required symbol, and all its descendants in the current retained set are removed? Actually it says \"deeper nodes before shallower nodes\" - so we process nodes from deepest to shallowest. For each candidate, we check if all required symbols are still represented after removal. If yes, we remove it (add to removed_node_ids) and continue.\n\nBut note that \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if any of its immediate children are retained. Since we process depth-first, deeper nodes will be considered first. If a child is removed, then the parent becomes candidate later (shallower) and may have no retained children (if all children removed). So the algorithm is: start with all nodes retained. For each node depth descending, if node is removable and not root and not a required symbol, and if all its children (in the original tree? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are currently retained in the current set) are not retained (i.e., have been removed), and if removing it does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining nodes, then remove it.\n\nBut careful: The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" must hold for the entire remaining set after removal. Since we are removing nodes, we need to ensure that the required symbols are still present in the remaining nodes. That means we cannot remove the last node that has a particular required symbol. So we need to maintain a count of how many nodes have each symbol. The required_symbols list may contain duplicates? It's a list; we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. (Probably each required symbol appears at least once in the tree; root is required.)\n\nEdge cases: The root might have a required symbol? In given examples, root symbol is 'root', which is in required_symbols, so root cannot be removed anyway. But if required_symbols include 'root', it's covered.\n\nNow, the output: \"removed_node_ids\" - list of node IDs in order of removal? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order we removed them (presumably the order determined by the algorithm: deeper first, tie-break ID). retained_node_ids should be sorted (probably lexicographically). The output schema requires both arrays of strings.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate payload: must have 'nodes' list, 'required_symbols' list. Each node must have required fields. Could raise ValueError if invalid.\n2. Build a mapping from node id to node dict. Also build parent-child relationships.\n3. Determine the set of required symbols. (Could be list, we check for 'root' special? No, just use as given.)\n4. Determine removable nodes: those with removable=True. But also root is not removable even if flag says removable? The description says \"Never remove the root\", so we must treat root as not removable regardless of flag. Similarly, nodes with required symbol? Actually \"a required symbol\" means nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So any node whose symbol is in required_symbols is not removable, even if its removable flag is True. So we need to consider that. Also nodes with retained children: we need to check dynamically as we remove.\n5. Create a set of retained node IDs initially all nodes. Also maintain a set of removed node IDs (empty).\n6. Also need to maintain for each symbol, a set of node IDs that have that symbol and are retained. Or count.\n7. Process nodes in order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (since tie-break by node ID). Use the provided depth field. Then for each node in that order:\n   - If node id not in retained set (already removed) -> skip.\n   - If node is root (parent None) -> skip.\n   - If node symbol is in required_symbols -> skip. (But careful: if the node's symbol matches a required symbol, we cannot remove it even if there are other nodes with that symbol? The condition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" suggests we cannot remove any node that itself has a symbol that is required. So even if there are other nodes with same symbol, we don't remove this one. That matches typical interpretation: the node itself is required. So we check: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols (set) then skip.\n   - If node['removable'] is False -> skip (but root and required already handled).\n   - Check if node has any retained child: iterate over children (list built from mapping) and if any child id is in retained set, then skip.\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to become absent: i.e., after removal, for each required symbol, there must be at least one node with that symbol remaining. We can precompute counts: for each symbol, count of retained nodes. When removing, decrement count for the node's symbol; if any count becomes zero for a required symbol, then cannot remove. If okay, remove.\n   - Add node id to removed_node_ids (append to list), remove from retained set, decrement count.\n8. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted list of retained set.\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_nodes_list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_list}.\n\nPotential issues: The order of processing: we go deepest first. That ensures we remove leaf nodes before their parents. Since we also check \"retained children\" condition, we must not remove a node if any child is retained. That will automatically be enforced because deeper nodes are processed first, so if a child is removed, when we later process the parent, it may have no retained children. But if a child is not removable or required, then parent cannot be removed because child is retained. That is correct.\n\nHowever, there might be scenarios where multiple children exist and some are removed, some not. Then parent cannot be removed because there is at least one retained child. That's fine.\n\nAlso need to consider nodes with no children (leaves) - condition passes. For internal nodes with all children removed, condition passes.\n\nNow, about the \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We check that.\n\nImportant: The required_symbols list may contain symbols that appear in multiple nodes. We need to preserve at least one occurrence of each. Since we never remove a node that itself is a required symbol, the only way a required symbol could disappear is if we remove all nodes with that symbol. That cannot happen because we check counts. However, we never remove a node whose symbol is required. So the only required symbols that could be removed are those that appear only in non-required nodes? Wait, required symbols are defined as list of symbols that must be present. But the nodes themselves are not marked as \"required nodes\"; the description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" meaning a node that has a symbol that is in required_symbols? Or does it mean a node that is required by the grammar? The phrase \"a required symbol\" is ambiguous. In Perses, \"required symbols\" are those that must appear in the reduced tree. So nodes with those symbols are crucial. The rule says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So I interpret that as: if a node's symbol is in the required_symbols list, then that node cannot be removed. So we must not remove it. Therefore, we only need to check that after removal of other nodes, the required symbols are still present in some nodes. Since we never remove a node with required symbol, the only way a required symbol could be lost is if all nodes with that symbol are removed. But those nodes are all non-required nodes. So we need to count for each required symbol, the number of retained nodes that have that symbol. If after removing a non-required node, the count for its symbol (which may be required) drops to zero, then we cannot remove it. But note: if the node itself has a required symbol, we don't even consider removal. So it's fine.\n\nThus algorithm stands.\n\nBut there is a nuance: The list 'required_symbols' might include symbols that are not present in any node? That would be infeasible, so we should raise ValueError. Similarly, if the root is not present, or tree is malformed.\n\nAlso need to handle that parent references might be null for root, else string. Build children mapping.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate the input case. The case is passed as argument; we should not modify it. We can copy data as needed.\n\nNow, we need to output Python source code for solve(case) as a string inside a JSON with key \"implementation\". The response must be exactly that JSON.\n\nWe'll produce the implementation function.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nEdge Cases: The payload may have additional keys like _case_nonce; we ignore. Nodes may have extra fields; we use id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Utility not used in algorithm? Possibly not. Depth is used for ordering. We must ensure that the depth field is consistent with the tree structure, but we rely on it.\n\nWe'll also handle cases where a node's parent is not present in the nodes list? That would be invalid.\n\nImplementation steps in code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    nodes = case.get(\"nodes\")\n    required_symbols = case.get(\"required_symbols\")\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case structure\")\n    # Build node dict and children\n    node_map = {}\n    parent_map = {}\n    for nd in nodes:\n        nid = nd.get(\"id\")\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"id must be string\")\n        node_map[nid] = nd\n        parent = nd.get(\"parent\")\n        parent_map[nid] = parent  # None for root\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for nid in node_map:\n        parent = parent_map[nid]\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent in children:\n                children[parent].append(nid)\n            else:\n                raise ValueError(f\"parent {parent} not found\")\n    # Check root exists\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, nd in node_map.items() if nd.get(\"parent\") is None]\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\n    # Required symbols validation: ensure each is present in at least one node\n    req_symbols_set = set(required_symbols)\n    all_symbols = {nd[\"symbol\"] for nd in node_map.values()}\n    for s in req_symbols_set:\n        if s not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {s} not present in any node\")\n    # Initialize retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []  # order of removal\n    # Count occurrences of each symbol among retained nodes\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for nd in node_map.values():\n        sym = nd[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then node ID ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda nd: (-nd[\"depth\"], nd[\"id\"]))\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = nd[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Skip root\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Skip if symbol is required (node itself has a required symbol)\n        if nd[\"symbol\"] in req_symbols_set:\n            continue\n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not nd.get(\"removable\", False):\n            continue\n        # Check if node has any retained child\n        has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if removing this node would cause a required symbol to become absent\n        # Decrease count of its symbol temporarily\n        sym = nd[\"symbol\"]\n        new_count = symbol_counts[sym] - 1\n        # Determine if any required symbol would become absent\n        # Only need to check if this symbol is required and would become zero\n        # But other symbols unaffected.\n        if sym in req_symbols_set and new_count == 0:\n            # Cannot remove because it would eliminate the required symbol\n            continue\n        # Otherwise, remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] = new_count\n    # Sort retained ids\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nBut note: The condition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might also apply to nodes whose symbol is required, but we already skip that. So it's fine.\n\nNow, consider the case where a node is removable and its symbol is not required, but after removal, the count of some other required symbol (not its own) might drop to zero? That can't happen because we only decrement count for its own symbol. Other symbols unchanged. So only its own symbol could become zero. So we only check if sym in req_symbols_set. Good.\n\nBut what about the situation where a node's symbol is not required, but removing it also removes a descendant that has a required symbol? Wait: We are removing the node itself, not its descendants. The descendants are separate nodes. If we remove a node, we don't remove its children automatically. The children remain as separate nodes. The algorithm only removes nodes one by one. So removing an ancestor node does not remove its children. The children are still retained, and they have their own symbols. So the required symbols are still present if children have them. That's correct. However, we must ensure that the tree structure after removal may have orphaned nodes (children without parent). That is acceptable because we are not maintaining tree structure; we just track retained nodes. The condition \"node with retained children\" prevents removal of a node that still has children, because those children are retained and would become disconnected. But in Perses, removing a node typically means also removing its subtree or replacing it. However, the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children have all been removed. That's what we implement.\n\nBut wait: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests we process deeper first. Our algorithm does that.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that we don't remove nodes that are required by the grammar in a different sense? The \"required_symbols\" list is given. The root might not be in required_symbols but it's still not removable. That's fine.\n\nNow, check against given fixtures. The first fixture: nodes list has root, n1 (depth1, removable false, symbol s1, required_symbols includes s1 so n1 is required, cannot be removed), n2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0). Required symbols: root, s1. So we must retain at least one node with symbol s1. n1 has s1 and is not removable. So that's fine. n4 also has s1 and is removable. n6 has s0 but not required. Process deeper first: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (depths 2). n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so we skip removal (never remove required symbol). n5 removable false, skip. n6 removable true, symbol s0 not required, no retained children? n6 has no children? In the list, no node has parent n6, so leaf. Is root or required? No. So we can remove n6 if all required symbols remain. Required symbols: root and s1. root is root, s1 is present in n1 (retained) and n4 (retained). Removing n6 does not affect counts of root or s1. So n6 can be removed. removed.append(\"n6\"). Then next depth1 nodes: n1 (required, skip), n2 (removable true, symbol s2 not required, check children: n4 is retained (since we haven't removed n4), so has retained child => cannot remove). n3 (removable false, skip). So no more removals. Then retained: all except n6. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] sorted. That matches expected? We don't have expected output, but likely.\n\nSecond fixture similar. Third. So code should work.\n\nNow, we need to output the implementation as JSON. The response must be a JSON object with key \"im